robert goddard is widely known as the  father of modern rocketry  he designed and launched the first  liquid-fueled rocket in 1926  and his designs are still used today but  he received little recognition while he  was alive  and was forced to work in secrecy  because of a series of critical articles  published in the new york times  journalists mocked his theories as  impossible  but he was right and he knew it he  stayed the course and is remembered  today as the man who ushered in the  space age  i'm john coogan and this is how robert  goddard beat the new york times  this story is more than 100 years old  now but it's more important than ever  especially for anyone working on new  technologies  robert goddard was born in 1882 and  spent most of his early life in  worcester massachusetts  this was just around the time that  american cities were first getting  electrified  and goddard became entranced by science  and technology  with so much incredible innovation  happening all around him on earth  he started wondering if it was possible  to escape earth's gravitational pull  and explore the stars at age 16  goddard read the war of the worlds by  h.g wells and was fascinated by the  ideas presented in the story  in the book aliens from mars invade  earth and a singular protagonist  played by tom cruise in the 2005  adaptation has to fight to survive  one year later on october 19 1899  goddard climbed a cherry tree to trim  dead limbs  while in the tree he gazed up at the  stars and became transfixed  he later wrote i imagined how wonderful  it would be to make some device  which even had the possibility of  ascending to mars  but building such a device wouldn't be  easy goddard was frail and sickly his  entire life  and suffered from a variety of illnesses  that caused him to fall two years behind  his classmates  critics thought it would be impossible  for humanity to ever reach space  and his struggles with traditional  educational pathways really set him back  but he was still determined to build the  rocket he had dreamt of in that cherry  tree  so goddard worked tirelessly spending  days reading scientific texts  while unable to attend school and  creating his own curricula  in service of his ultimate goal space  exploration  his private studies eventually started  to pay off and he gained recognition for  his sharp scientific mind  he was able to earn advanced degrees in  physics and eventually landed a research  fellowship at princeton in 1913 but his  work there was cut short by a terrible  bout of tuberculosis  doctors expected him to die and goddard  thought death was a real possibility as  well  so he scribbled notes as clearly as he  could while he was recovering in the  hopes  that someone would be able to interpret  them if he were to die  after fighting tooth and nail to recover  goddard spent the next six years  consolidating his research into a single  coherent work  in 1919 he published a method of  reaching extreme altitudes in the  smithsonian  the paper outlined a number of important  strategies that would allow a rocket to  escape the gravitational pull of earth  first among them was the use of a steam  turbine nozzle to more efficiently  convert  the energy of hot gases into forward  motion goddard anticipated that critics  wouldn't believe his results  even if he launched a rocket  successfully once that rocket  left visual range there would be no way  for people to see how high the rocket  had gone  in order to counter these critics  goddard proposed sending a payload of  flash powder to the moon  to set off an explosion bright enough to  see from earth with an ordinary  telescope  this would allow any skeptic to  independently verify that the rocket had  indeed reached the moon  by watching the flash powder explode but  this idea backfired on him  instead of persuading critics of the  validity of his ideas  it made them think he was just a madman  who wanted to blow up the moon  the new york times published a scathing  editorial titled a severe strain on  credulity  and said quote after the rocket quits  our air and really starts on its longer  journey  its flight would be neither accelerated  nor maintained by the explosion of the  charges it then  might have left this criticism was based  on the then common belief  that thrust was produced by the rocket  exhaust pushing against the atmosphere  but that's not how rockets work it's  actually newton's third law  for every action there is an equal and  opposite reaction  that allows rockets to propel themselves  in space essentially  the rocket is not pushing against the  air in the atmosphere but against itself  fuel exits the bottom of the rocket and  pushes against the structure of the  rocket to accelerate upward  interestingly this misconception is  actually at the heart of many moon  landing conspiracies  but you can disprove this idea yourself  using widely available equipment  just like the youtubers over at warped  perception did  they built a vacuum chamber using some  plexiglass and an air compressor to suck  all the air out  once the vacuum was sealed they lit some  rocket fuel and observed the force it  applied to a scale  to prove that even without air to push  against  rockets can still create propellant  force  goddard tried to win over his skeptics  by publishing rebuttals in popular  science magazine  and the associated press in which he  outlined vacuum tests he had performed  to verify his claims but he was still  widely mocked  since he was still so early in the  development of rocketry most of his  experiments fell well short of their  intended goal  far from reaching the moon his rockets  would often explode a few hundred feet  in the air  but these tests were incredibly  important in understanding the mechanics  of his theories in practice  ultimately goddard realized that the  only way to achieve his goal of space  flight was to ignore the critics and  just build  so that's what he did from 1926 to 1941  he launched 35 rockets and reached  several important milestones  he repeatedly broke altitude records all  while implementing groundbreaking  features like liquid propellant  gyroscopic controls and multiple thrust  chambers  with his dedication finally paying off  goddard kept setting his sights higher  and higher  never taking his eye off the prize of  escaping earth's gravity  he formed critical partnerships with the  famous aviator charles lindbergh  and won financing from harry guggenheim  but goddard had changed  the intense criticism he had received in  the 20s led him to live a life of  secrecy  refraining from publishing his work  until every theory had been thoroughly  demonstrated in practice  this hurt his ability to collaborate  with other rocket scientists at the time  and ultimately slowed the pace of  development in the united states broadly  nearly 50 years after the new york times  published their original criticism of  robert goddard's ideas  they issued a correction it was july 17  1969 and nasa had just launched apollo  11  with three american astronauts on board  to land on the moon  no one could deny that humanity could  escape the earth with the right  technology  and the new york times had to correct  their air so how  exactly did robert goddard beat the new  york times he focused on what was  irrefutable  the laws of physics and the rocket  science that he had proven  both mathematically and experimentally  and that's an incredibly important  lesson for any entrepreneur working on  something new  there will always be skeptics looking to  maintain the status quo  and refusing to imagine a brighter  future but they must be ignored  goddard knew this intimately and summed  it up best when a reporter asked him  about the new york times criticism  he said every vision is a joke until the  first man accomplishes it  once realized it becomes commonplace and  that rings true  more than ever today if you like this  video please consider subscribing to the  channel  and if you'd like to get in touch with  me just leave me a comment below or  reach out to me on twitter  thanks for watching
 

